# PHASE 6 — Node System Overhaul: Deep Customization

**Read `00 - START HERE` and finish Phase 4 first.** This phase does not depend on Phase 5 and can run in a separate agent session in parallel with it if you want to move faster — nothing here touches new routes, only the builder's node/config system.

This is the core of what you actually asked for: every node goes from "a handful of sliders and selects" to "every setting a real implementation would need," with a Deep Customization popup that exposes all of it, while the Inspector's right-side panel stays clean and shows only the essentials. Everything below is still a frontend/mock exercise — no real model API is called anywhere in this phase. What changes is the *shape* of the config data and the UI that edits it, so that when a real backend shows up later, the config it needs to read already exists in a sane form.

---

## 1. Where this lives today, exactly

`mock/layers.ts` defines two things that matter here:
- `FieldDef` — a discriminated union with 7 variants: `text`, `password`, `select`, `slider`, `switch`, `checklist`, `number`.
- `ComponentDef` — has one `fields: FieldDef[]` array, rendered by `components/builder/inspector.tsx`, which switches on `field.type` and renders the matching input, reading/writing through `useBuilder`'s `updateNodeConfig`.

`lib/workspace-store.ts` has `defaultConfig(comp)` (builds the initial `config` object from each field's `value`) and `computeNeedsConfig(comp, config)` (currently: true if any `text`/`password` field is empty — this is the whole validation story today).

That's the entire system. Six field types, one flat array, one panel. Everything below extends this without breaking it.

