## 4. Split every `ComponentDef` into basic vs. advanced, and add real documentation content

Extend `ComponentDef` in `mock/layers.ts`:
```
export interface ComponentDef {
  // ...all existing fields stay exactly as they are...
  advancedFields?: FieldDef[]
  docs?: {
    whenToUse: string
    whenToSkip: string
    bestPractices: string[]
    commonMistakes: string[]
  }
}
```
`fields` (existing) = what the Inspector's right panel shows, always — keep these genuinely minimal, 2–4 fields, the "if you only change one thing" settings. `advancedFields` = everything else, shown only inside the Deep Customization modal (§6). `docs` backs the modal's Documentation tab and the Library detail page from Phase 5 §4 (if you're running Phase 5 and 6 in parallel, both should read from this same field once it exists — don't build two separate documentation data shapes).

**Retrofit order** — don't try to write `advancedFields`/`docs`/a `model-select` for all 68 components in one pass. Do it in this order, stopping to verify each group renders correctly in both the Inspector and the new modal before moving to the next:

1. **Intelligence Agents layer (6 components)** — first and most important, since this is the layer the "GPT/Gemini/Claude/Qwen/DeepSeek/Ollama" ask is really about. For each of `technical-agent`, `sentiment-agent`, `macro-agent`, `flow-agent`, `contrarian-agent`, `event-agent`: keep the existing 1–2 `fields` as basic, and add to `advancedFields`:
   - a `model-select` field (`key: 'model'`)
   - a `prompt` field (`key: 'systemPrompt'`) pre-filled with a real, specific default prompt reflecting that agent's persona — e.g. for `technical-agent`: *"You are a technical analyst. Given the feature vector for {{symbol}}, form a directional view based on price structure, momentum, and volume. State your confidence 0–1 and a one-sentence rationale."* Write a similarly specific, non-generic default for each of the other five agents based on their existing `description`/`tagline` — this is exactly what your original ask meant by "the website will already have very nice setup ready... the user will be 100% allowed to edit anything."
   - a `slider` for output confidence threshold if the agent doesn't already have one.
   Add `docs` for all 6 using their existing `description`/`useCase` as source material, expanded into the four-part shape above.

2. **Debate layer (whichever components exist there — `bull-bear`, `moderator`, and any others in `mock/layers.ts`)** — same treatment, since debate nodes are also LLM-reasoning-backed. Their `systemPrompt` defaults should reflect their structural role (e.g. `moderator`: *"You are scoring a debate between a bull and bear case. Weigh argument quality, not just confidence. Output a winner and a blended confidence score."*).

3. **Everything else** — once steps 1–2 are verified working end-to-end (Inspector shows basic fields, modal shows everything, config saves and reloads correctly), extend `advancedFields`/`docs` to the remaining layers. Most non-agent nodes (data feeds, indicators, risk rules, execution) don't need a `model-select` at all — they need `advancedFields` with the kind of settings a real implementation would expose (e.g. `ohlcv-feed` advanced: a `key-value` field for custom exchange headers, a `credential` for the data provider's API key currently hiding in the flat `Settings` page instead of on the node where it belongs — see §7). Don't force a model picker onto nodes that have no business having one; use judgment per node, guided by its existing `description`.

