## 5. Input variable mapping (so `{{variables}}` in a prompt actually mean something)

A node's real inputs come from whatever's wired into it on the canvas, not from a fixed schema — e.g. a `moderator` node might have two, three, or four `Signal` edges feeding it depending on the graph. For the `prompt` field type's variable-insertion affordance to be meaningful, it needs to know what's actually connected right now, not just a generic list.

In `components/builder/inspector.tsx` (or wherever the modal ends up living), compute available variables for the selected node at render time: `edges.filter(e => e.target === node.id)`, and for each, look up the source node's `ComponentDef` name to build a default label (e.g. `bull_case`, `bear_case` if two `Signal`-typed edges come from nodes whose names suggest that framing — simplest correct approach: default the variable name to a slugified version of the source component's `name`, and let the user rename it). Store user-given labels in the node's `config` under a reserved key, e.g. `config.__inputLabels: Record<sourceNodeId, string>`, edited via a small "Input variables" section at the top of the Deep Customization modal's Model & Prompt tab — one row per real incoming edge, an editable label `Input` next to each, feeding the "insert variable" list in the `prompt` field.

This is the piece that makes "all settings compatible with each other" real rather than cosmetic: the prompt editor should only ever offer variables that are actually wired in, and if a saved prompt references a `{{variable}}` that no longer exists (edge was deleted since), flag it — add a new check to `lib/validate.ts`: `warning` level, "Prompt references {{x}} but no input provides it," pointing at that node id, using the same `Issue` shape (`id`, `level`, `title`, `detail`, `nodeIds`) every other check in that file already uses.

