## 6. The Deep Customization modal

Build `components/builder/deep-customization-dialog.tsx`. Large `Dialog` (override the default max-width — go close to full-viewport, this needs room; check `DialogContent`'s className prop for a size override, same as how `UnlockDialog`/the block-save dialog already pass a custom `max-w-*`). Structure as a `Tabs`/`TabsList` inside the dialog body:

- **Configuration** — every field from both `fields` and `advancedFields` together, grouped under two headers ("Basic" / "Advanced") using the same field-rendering logic as the Inspector (see refactor note below) — this tab alone already delivers "see everything, edit everything."
- **Model & Prompt** — only present when the component has a `model-select` field. The input-variable-mapping mini-section from §5, then the `model-select` field, then the `prompt` field full-width and taller than it renders in a cramped side panel (this modal is exactly where a prompt editor deserves real space — give it a `min-h-64` textarea, not the compact one-line-visible default).
- **Documentation** — renders `comp.docs` (when-to-use / when-to-skip / best practices / common mistakes as bulleted lists) plus the existing `useCase` line, and a "View full library entry" link to `/app/library/${comp.id}` (Phase 5) if that page exists yet, otherwise omit the link rather than pointing at a 404.
- **Advanced/Reset** — a "Reset to defaults" button (`ConfirmDialog`-gated, calls `defaultConfig(comp)` and overwrites this node's `config`), and a small "Export config as JSON" / "Import config from JSON" pair (copy-to-clipboard and paste-to-apply, using the browser Clipboard API — this is a genuinely useful power-user feature for sharing one node's exact setup with someone else, and it's cheap to build since `config` is already a plain serializable object).

**Refactor requirement, do this as part of building the modal, not as an afterthought:** the Inspector currently has its field-rendering `switch` inline in `inspector.tsx`. Extract it into `components/builder/field-renderer.tsx` exporting a single `renderField(field: FieldDef, value: unknown, onChange: (v: unknown) => void)` function (or a `<FieldRenderer />` component, whichever fits the codebase's existing conventions better — check how similar shared-rendering utilities are structured elsewhere, e.g. `LEVEL_META` in `console-panel.tsx` for the "lookup table of render logic" pattern already in use). Both the Inspector's basic panel and this modal's Configuration tab call the same function. Extending `FieldDef` in the future should only ever require touching this one file's switch statement, never both the Inspector and the modal separately.

**Trigger**: add a "Deep customization" button to the bottom of the Inspector's field list, below existing fields, above the "Docs" link — full-width, `variant="secondary"`, a `Sparkles` or `SlidersHorizontal` icon, opens this dialog for the currently-selected node.

**Wire the onboarding "experience level" from Phase 5 §1, if you built it**: `experience === 'beginner'` still shows the "Deep customization" button, just with slightly less visual weight (ghost variant instead of secondary) — never hide it outright, a beginner should still be able to find it, just not have it competing for attention immediately.

